[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.401478Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure1 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 1: Two multimodal learning paradigms studied in this work. (Left) Cross-prediction (CP), Equation (2): an encoder fX maps modality x to a latent code z, and a decoder fD reconstructs the paired target y. (Right) Crossalignment (CA), Equation (1): encoders fX and fY project paired samples (xi,yi) into a shared latent space where matched pairs are pulled together." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.401916Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure2 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 2: Phase diagram for signal recovery in (κ, ν) space under the homogeneous model (all signal and noise components are equal). Solid and dashed lines respectively show the ∆CA = 1 and ∆CP = 1 boundaries from Proposition 3.1. (a) Large target nuisance (γ\u{303}y ≫ γy). (b) Small target noise (γ\u{303}y ∼ γy). Phase diagrams for the non-homogeneous case with partial recoveries are shown in Figure 7." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.402245Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure3 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 3: Recovery as a function of normalized noise correlation ν. (Left) Subspace distance between the estimated and true signal subspace (lower is better; shading shows ±1 std over 20 trials). CP fails at ν ≈ 0.15 while CA remains robust until ν ≈ 0.75. (Right) Theoretical separation ratios ∆CA and ∆CP (log scale). The dashed line marks ∆ = 1; recovery succeeds above this threshold." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.402642Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure4 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 4: Linear probe accuracy vs. nuisance alignment νmax = 1 − σjitter. (Left) Stereo-dSprites (3-class, grayscale, 100k samples). (Right) Stereo-3DShapes (4-class, RGB, 100k samples). In both settings, color represents weak noise levels. In both panels, the trade-off between the methods is clearly seen. CA (solid, circles) peaks at moderate-to-low alignment and collapses at full alignment; CP (dashed, squares) shows the opposite pattern. The crossover at νmax ≈ 0.8 is consistent across datasets. Lower absolute ceilings in 3DShapes reflect the harder discrimination task." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.402912Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure5 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 5: UMAP embeddings of learned representations of the stereo-dSprites experiment (color = shape, intensity = position). From left to right: CA with aligned noise (σjitter = 0), CA with misaligned noise (σjitter = 0.5), CP with aligned noise, and CP with misaligned noise. All experiments have the same modality-specific noise (σnoise = 0.5). Each method succeeds exactly where the other fails, and on failures, the models learn the nuisance." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.403202Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure6 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 6: Top-1 accuracy vs. image style transform strength for MSCOCO experiment. CP shows an asymmetric nature: prediction of image from text results in similar performance as CA but prediction of text from an image results in much better performance. Both approaches converge to the same accuracy when image noise is high." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.403576Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure7 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 7: Partial recovery under heterogeneous signal spectra. Empirical recovery count r (number of signal directions with squared projection ≥ 0.8 onto the top-k recovered subspace) across the (κ\u{304}, ν) plane, for signal spread ρ ∈ {1.0, 0.8, 0.6, 0.4} (columns, homogeneous → heterogeneous). (Top): CA. (Bottom): CP. Signal strengths follow a geometric decay κi = κ\u{304} ρ i−1 with k = 5, d = 20; noise parameters γx = 1, γy = 0.05, γ\u{303}y = 5. Overlaid curves: ∆CA = 1 (solid) and ∆CP = 1 (dashed) from the homogeneous theory of figure 2. At ρ = 1 the transition is sharp (r ∈ {0, k}) and the ∆ = 1 contours align with the empirical boundary, reproducing figure 2. As ρ decreases, intermediate counts r ∈ {1, . . . , k − 1} fill a widening band in which stronger signal directions are recovered first; the four-region phase diagram smears into a graded continuum. Averaged over 10 seeds with n = 5,000." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.403903Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure8 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 8: Separation ratios ∆CA and ∆CP as a function of target nuisance variance γ\u{303}y, validating Proposition 3.1. Theory curves (dashed) are computed from the closed-form expressions in Theorems A.1 and A.2; empirical curves (solid) are estimated from finite-sample covariances averaged over 20 random rotations. As γ\u{303}y grows, ∆CA increases unboundedly — CA’s symmetric whitening suppresses high-variance nuisance on both sides — while ∆CP remains approximately constant, since ξj = ηj/ √ γ\u{303}xj does not depend on target nuisance variance: source-side whitening operates only on the source modality. Theory and empirical curves are in close agreement throughout, confirming the accuracy of the closed-form predictions at finite sample sizes." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
[2m2026-08-20T06:52:35.404246Z[0m [33m WARN[0m [2mda::load_journey[0m[2m:[0m [3marticle_id[0m[2m=[0m"2606.11190" [3martifact_id[0m[2m=[0mFigure9 [3mreason[0m[2m=[0mno section contains the figure caption "Figure 9: The variance trap: signal recovery vs. reconstruction quality for CA+Probe and direct CP, using the same parameter regime as Figure 3 (κ = (3.0, 2.0, 1.5), γ\u{303}y = 50.0, γ\u{303}x = 1.0). Green shading marks the regime where ∆CA > 1 > ∆CP. (a) Signal prediction MSE: in the green region, CA+Probe achieves near-zero signal MSE while CP’s signal error climbs steeply, confirming that CP encodes the wrong subspace. (b) Total prediction MSE: CP achieves lower total MSE than CA+Probe across the same region, because it successfully reconstructs the high-variance nuisance components — a task at which CA+Probe, having discarded nuisance directions, cannot compete. Together, the two panels illustrate the core danger of using reconstruction error as a proxy for signal recovery: CP can achieve lower loss than CA while failing to recover the signal, precisely because the MSE objective does not distinguish signal from nuisance." [3mevent[0m[2m=[0m"da_load_journey_figure_grounding_skipped"
